  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH NEXT MONDAY/...  \nThe initial ridge Tuesday will be the starting point for a \npersistent anti-cyclonic setup through Thanksgiving and the ensuing \nweekend. An unseasonably strong area of high pressure is shown \nbuilding to the west of the Baja Peninsula and expanding across much \nof the western CONUS during the back-half of the week. This will \nkeep mostly quiet conditions in the forecast during the extended \nperiod, resulting in what looks to be a wonderful holiday timeframe \nweather-wise. Another round of breeziness Thursday, this time \nfocused over the lower Colorado River Valley, will once again be the \ndeviant factor to the overall benign setup as the strong high \npressure interacts with the previously mentioned shortwave that will \nbe over Northern Mexico by this point in time.\n\nAfternoon highs by Thanksgiving will rise far enough to be \ncomfortably above normal values for the back-end of November, but \nthat will only result in middle to upper 70s becoming more common \nacross lower elevation areas. There might be a few spots, mainly out \nin SW Arizona and SE California, that reach into the lower 80s by \nthe weekend due to their closer proximity to the greatest height \nanomalies, but that appears to be the upper limit of how far \ntemperatures will rise. With the high centered to our southwest, \ncool northerly flow will likely be an inhibiting factor as to why we \nwill not see warmth that is completely out of the ordinary for this \ntime of year.",
